#! mathcas 2 notebook

#% title
# jacobi_cn

#% text
# `jacobi_cn(u, m)` gives the Jacobi elliptic function `cn(u | m)`, the cosine of `jacobi_amplitude(u, m)`.

#% section
# Details

#% item
# `cn` has the real period `4*K(m)`; `cn(u | 0)` is `cos(u)` and `cn(u | 1)` is `sech(u)` (DLMF 22.2, 22.5).

#% item
# Real machine numbers and balls give numerical values. `jacobi_cn` is listable.

#% section
# Examples

#% subsection
# Basic examples

#% cell
jacobi_cn(1.0, 0.5)
#% output
#= 0.5959765676721407

#% cell
jacobi_cn(u, 0)
#% output
#= cos(u)

#% cell
jacobi_cn(0.5, 0.9)
#% output
#= 0.8859187959416738

#% subsection
# Applications

#% text
# The angle of a pendulum released at 90 degrees, `2*asin(k*sn(K - t | m))`, at a few times (units with `g/L = 1`):

#% cell
table(2*asin(sqrt(0.5)*jacobi_cn(t, 0.5)), t, [0.0, 0.5, 1.0])
#% output
#= [1.5707963267948968, 1.3473352057145445, 0.8700191230969488]

#% subsection
# Properties and relations

#% text
# The derivative:

#% cell
d(jacobi_cn(u, m), u)
#% output
#= -jacobi_dn(u, m)*jacobi_sn(u, m)

#% text
# The quarter period is a zero:

#% cell
jacobi_cn(elliptic_k(0.3), 0.3)
#% output
#= 7.655061253971189e-17

#% section
# See also

#% text
# `jacobi_sn`, `jacobi_dn`, `jacobi_amplitude`, `inverse_jacobi_cn`
